  "logger": "AerospaceNU flight computer board",
  "notes": [
    "`baro1_pres` read in atmospheres, from its size and its step: on the pad it reads 0.9707179866765359, within the 0.5 to 1.1 that only atmospheres and bars of the units a barometer is written in put a pad, and each of its 73 cells written precisely enough to test is a whole number of pascals over 101,325 within a millionth of a step, as a barometer's pascals written in atmospheres are and in bars, over 100,000, aren't",
    "`baro2_pres` read in atmospheres, from its size and its step: on the pad it reads 0.9707475943745374, within the 0.5 to 1.1 that only atmospheres and bars of the units a barometer is written in put a pad, and each of its 73 cells written precisely enough to test is a whole number of pascals over 101,325 within a millionth of a step, as a barometer's pascals written in atmospheres are and in bars, over 100,000, aren't",
    "`baro_pres_avg` read in atmospheres, from its size and its step: on the pad it reads 0.9707327905255366, within the 0.5 to 1.1 that only atmospheres and bars of the units a barometer is written in put a pad, and each of its 73 cells written precisely enough to test is a whole or half number of pascals over 101,325, as an average of two whole ones is within a millionth of a step, as a barometer's pascals written in atmospheres are and in bars, over 100,000, aren't",
    "`timestamp_ms` read in milliseconds, from its spacing and the climb: its rows' median step is 25 as written, and the climb the pressure reads from the launch, over the one the raw accelerometer integrates to in its pad reading's 1 g, is, read in milliseconds, 1.051, and read in seconds, 0.000; a row every 5 to 500 ms, as the board logs, and a ratio within a factor of 10 of 1 are taken",
    "`imu1_accel_x_real`, `imu1_accel_y_real` and `imu1_accel_z_real` read in meters per second squared, from the pad's gravity and the sensor's scale: at rest they read 9.806, 1 g in that unit within 10%, and each is its raw count times 0.0071785, in that unit one of the counts of STMicroelectronics' LSM9DS1 (0.061, 0.122, 0.244 or 0.732 mg)",
    "`imu2_accel_x_real`, `imu2_accel_y_real` and `imu2_accel_z_real` read in meters per second squared, from the pad's gravity and the sensor's scale: at rest they read 9.820, 1 g in that unit within 10%, and each is its raw count times 0.0071785, in that unit one of the counts of STMicroelectronics' LSM9DS1 (0.061, 0.122, 0.244 or 0.732 mg)",
    "`high_g_accel_x_real`, `high_g_accel_y_real` and `high_g_accel_z_real` kept as written: the pad's gravity and the sensor's scale don't both bear one unit out: at rest they read 14.386, 1 g within 10% in neither m/s² nor g; each is its raw count times 0.0294200, none of the LSM9DS1's counts in either unit",
    "`imu_accel_x_avg`, `imu_accel_y_avg` and `imu_accel_z_avg` read in meters per second squared, from the pad's gravity and the IMUs: at rest they read 9.813, 1 g in that unit within 10%, and each is the mean of the IMUs' scaled columns, read in it",
    "`acc_x`, `acc_y` and `acc_z` read in meters per second squared, from the pad's gravity and the climb: at rest they read 9.813, and the pressure's climb from the launch over the one they integrate to is, read in m/s², 1.051, and read in g, 0.088; 1 g within 10% and a ratio within a factor of 1.8 of 1 are taken",
    "`imu1_gyro_x_real`, `imu1_gyro_y_real` and `imu1_gyro_z_real` read in radians per second, from the board's turning and the sensor's scale: over 3 stretches of 10 steps at most 0.05 s apart with the accelerometer within 15% of 1 g, its direction turns 0.600 rad, and the rates turn the board across it 0.600 rad read in radians per second, 0.010 rad read in degrees; the unit whose turn is within a quarter to 4 times the accelerometer's is taken, from 3 stretches and 0.2 rad; and each is its raw count times 0.0003054, in that unit one of the counts of STMicroelectronics' LSM9DS1 (8.75, 17.5 or 70 m°/s)",
    "`imu2_gyro_x_real`, `imu2_gyro_y_real` and `imu2_gyro_z_real` read in radians per second, from the board's turning and the sensor's scale: over 3 stretches of 10 steps at most 0.05 s apart with the accelerometer within 15% of 1 g, its direction turns 0.600 rad, and the rates turn the board across it 0.600 rad read in radians per second, 0.010 rad read in degrees; the unit whose turn is within a quarter to 4 times the accelerometer's is taken, from 3 stretches and 0.2 rad; and each is its raw count times 0.0003054, in that unit one of the counts of STMicroelectronics' LSM9DS1 (8.75, 17.5 or 70 m°/s)",
    "`imu_gyro_x_avg`, `imu_gyro_y_avg` and `imu_gyro_z_avg` read in radians per second, from the board's turning and the IMUs: over 3 stretches of 10 steps at most 0.05 s apart with the accelerometer within 15% of 1 g, its direction turns 0.600 rad, and the rates turn the board across it 0.600 rad read in radians per second, 0.010 rad read in degrees; the unit whose turn is within a quarter to 4 times the accelerometer's is taken, from 3 stretches and 0.2 rad; and each is the mean of the IMUs' scaled columns, read in it",
    "`gps_alt` read in meters, from the pressure's climb and the accelerometer's: its climb at the GPS's fresh fixes up to the apogee over the pressure's in the standard atmosphere is 1.000 as written, and its climb from the launch over the one the accelerometer integrates to is 1.050; a gain within 25% of 1 and a ratio within a factor of 1.8 are taken",
    "`pos_z` read in meters, from the pressure's climb and the accelerometer's: its climb from the pad to its highest over the pressure's in the standard atmosphere is 1.000 as written, and its climb from the launch over the one the accelerometer integrates to is 1.050; a gain within 25% of 1 and a ratio within a factor of 1.8 are taken",
    "`vel_z` read in meters per second, from `pos_z` and the accelerometer: over the steps at most 0.05 s long, `pos_z`'s change in meters is 1.000 times what it integrates to as written, and from the launch it is 1.000 times the speed the accelerometer integrates to; a slope within 25% of 1 and a ratio within a factor of 1.8 are taken",
    "`gps_lat` and `gps_long` read in NMEA 0183's degrees and minutes, ddmm.mmmm, from their values and their companions: they pass 90 or 180, which no degrees do, and every one's last two digits before the point, its minutes, are under 60, and `gps_lat_mod` and `gps_long_mod` hold them converted from degrees and minutes to degrees",
    "`gps_lat_mod` and `gps_long_mod` read in degrees, from `gps_lat` and `gps_long`: each is its companion, read in degrees and minutes, converted to degrees",
